\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces What the composition tie-break does at product level, for the trace-element products. Each row is one product code in the FDA listing, with its structured active substances and the market key the normalisation assigns it. The two four-element products carry different substance sets and land in different keys, which is the tie-break working as described; the five-element product is a separate market on its name. The full table for these and for 1{,}493\xspace  electrolyte and trace-element product codes is released as \texttt  {results/ndc\_composition\_audit.csv}, with the raw name, the route, the complete substance string and the raw market keys. What the tie-break cannot do is measured there too, in four mutually exclusive categories and split by panel membership: of 326\xspace  name-and-bucket combinations, 5\xspace  carry two or more different populated substance sets, 1\xspace  carries one populated set alongside records with none, 47\xspace  have none at all, and 273\xspace  are consistent. All 5\xspace  conflicts fall among the 627\xspace  product codes that have no market in the panel; 0\xspace  of the 97\xspace  panel-linked groups carry one. 0\xspace  product codes resolve to more than one market key.}}{38}{table.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ndc}{{12}{38}{What the composition tie-break does at product level, for the trace-element products. Each row is one product code in the FDA listing, with its structured active substances and the market key the normalisation assigns it. The two four-element products carry different substance sets and land in different keys, which is the tie-break working as described; the five-element product is a separate market on its name. The full table for these and for \nNdcCodes electrolyte and trace-element product codes is released as \texttt {results/ndc\_composition\_audit.csv}, with the raw name, the route, the complete substance string and the raw market keys. What the tie-break cannot do is measured there too, in four mutually exclusive categories and split by panel membership: of \nNdcCombos name-and-bucket combinations, \nNdcMulti carry two or more different populated substance sets, \nNdcMixed carries one populated set alongside records with none, \nNdcAllMissing have none at all, and \nNdcClean are consistent. All \nNdcOutConflicts conflicts fall among the \nNdcUnassigned product codes that have no market in the panel; \nNdcPanelConflicts of the \nNdcAssignedGroups panel-linked groups carry one. \nNdcMultiKey product codes resolve to more than one market key}{table.12}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Route coverage by year, as a share of dispensing volume in the national panel. Records whose FDA listing carries no \texttt  {ROUTENAME} are passed to the form-string cascade described in Section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {subsec:market}, and only those matching none of its tests are assigned to \textsc  {other} rather than dropped. This table reports how much dispensing volume in each year carries an explicit route and is therefore unaffected by that fallback.}}{39}{table.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:routecov}{{13}{39}{Route coverage by year, as a share of dispensing volume in the national panel. Records whose FDA listing carries no \texttt {ROUTENAME} are passed to the form-string cascade described in Section~\ref {subsec:market}, and only those matching none of its tests are assigned to \textsc {other} rather than dropped. This table reports how much dispensing volume in each year carries an explicit route and is therefore unaffected by that fallback}{table.13}{}}
